{
  "id": "Compound::DB01151 - Gene::1133",
  "verbalization": "Desipramine, a tricyclic antidepressant, is shown to bind to Chrm4, a receptor involved in the activation of G-protein coupled acetylcholine receptor activity or muscarinic acetylcholine receptors. These pathways are central to the modulation of cholinergic signaling, which is implicated in various physiological and pathological processes. The evidence suggests that Desipramine may influence Chrm5 through a shared intermediary pathway involving G-protein coupled receptors, as Chrm5 is part of the muscarinic acetylcholine receptor family. The consistent binding to Chrm4 and the participation of Chrm4 in receptor activity pathways indicate a potential mechanism by which Desipramine could modulate Chrm5 function. The overlapping roles of Chrm4 in different receptor activities highlight the importance of G-protein coupled receptors in the pharmacological effects of Desipramine, suggesting a broader impact on cholinergic signaling.",
